  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "not stated",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests generalization of discrete prompts on pretrained vision-language models, including random-label fitting; this is a new setting outside the listed claim versions' model and training recipes.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports results on prompt generalization, prompt length, random-label fitting, and small-data search. These do not test the listed versions' standard-gradient network conditions; record them under fits_no_version. Figure-only results without a textual numeric value are described in the paper but not extracted as numeric rows.",
    "test_reason": "The study measures discrete prompt search with frozen pretrained CLIP encoders, rather than any V1–V8 intervention or comparison. Its random-label experiments concern the capacity of prompt search, not fitting network weights under V1's training recipes. They therefore do not challenge V1. The study discusses the original findings but tests a different generalization question.",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors conclude that discrete prompt engineering and greedy search often generalize well on pretrained vision-language models, and that classical PAC-Bayes bounds with an LLM prior can capture this behavior. They explicitly exclude explaining the underlying pretrained model's generalization. Formal validity also assumes that the image encoder was not trained on the downstream prompt-training data, which they cannot verify for CLIP.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Discrete prompt search or handcrafted prompts on pretrained CLIP image and text encoders, analyzed using PAC-Bayes bounds; not standard gradient training of networks from scratch.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Prompt length: the authors report that the generalization gap increases with length. Search vocabulary and training-data proportion are also varied; text describes limited error increases with smaller data or vocabulary. Flipped-label proportion changes train and test accuracy in tandem.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Table 2 reports train/test errors for greedy discrete prompts on frozen CLIP encoders, including 0.023/0.028 on CIFAR-10 and 0.142/0.180 on CIFAR-100 with L-14. It also evaluates handcrafted prompts.",
        "proposed_claim": "Discrete prompt search on pretrained vision-language models can achieve low held-out error with relatively small train-test gaps.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R1",
          "R2",
