        ]
      },
      {
        "finding": "Sections 5 and B and Figures 6 and 10 report that greedy search with prompt length five does not fit randomly flipped labels on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, fMoW, and OfficeHome; training and test accuracy deteriorate together.",
        "proposed_claim": "The tested discrete prompt-search procedure on frozen CLIP encoders has limited capacity to fit random labels.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Figure 5 reports increasing generalization gaps with prompt length, while training accuracy eventually plateaus and the authors describe little overfitting over the tested lengths.",
        "proposed_claim": "Increasing discrete prompt length can increase the train-test gap without producing severe overfitting in the tested setting.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Section 5 reports less than a two-percentage-point error increase using 2% of CIFAR-10 training data. With only 20 samples per class, Table 3 reports greedy train/test errors of 0.020/0.138 on CIFAR-10 and 0.156/0.367 on CIFAR-100.",
        "proposed_claim": "Discrete prompt search can work with limited labeled data, but its generalization gap grows in very small samples.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Table 2 gives an ImageNet PAC-Bayes error bound of 0.319 versus observed test error of 0.256. The bounds use a language-model prior over prompts rather than weight perturbations or magnitude-aware sharpness.",
        "proposed_claim": "A language-model prior over discrete prompts can yield comparatively tight PAC-Bayes bounds, conditional on independence from downstream training data.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R25",
          "R26"
        ]
      }
    ],
